From flom at ndri.org  Thu May  1 18:43:09 2003
From: flom at ndri.org (Peter Flom)
Date: Thu, 01 May 2003 14:43:09 -0400
Subject: Bootstrap question - very small N in a group
Message-ID: <seb13293.021@MAIL.NDRI.ORG>

Hello

My boss is doing some analyses comparing two groups on a continuous
variable.  In one case, the n in one of the groups is 4 (total N is
120).

A regular t-test was highly significant (for the larger group, n = 116,
mean = 2.3 with an sd of 1.7; for the smaller group, n = 4, with a mean
of 4.3 and an sd of 0.5)

He asked me to bootstrap these results, since he was concerned
(correctly, IMHO) about the robustness of the t test with such different
sample sizes and different variances.  

I suggested that doing the comparison was probably not a good idea,
even with bootstrap.....but he wants it done.

So I ran

bootCOMEV <- bootstrap (data = peter, statistic =
coef(lm(hlthact2~COMEV)), B = 1000)

and got an error that the computed fit was singular.  I think this
means that one of the resamples could not compute a regression since
none of the smaller group of people were included.  

Is there any way around this?

Peter

