From timh at insightful.com  Thu May  1 19:40:09 2003
From: timh at insightful.com (Tim Hesterberg)
Date: Thu, 01 May 2003 12:40:09 -0700
Subject: Re: Bootstrap question - very small N in a group
In-Reply-To: <seb13293.021@MAIL.NDRI.ORG>
References: <seb13293.021@MAIL.NDRI.ORG>
Message-ID: <200305011940.MAA13568@tomato.insightful.com>

(1) Use a permutation test, not bootstrapping
(2) When the explanatory variable is dichotomous, then instead
of doing a regression do a permutation test of the difference
in means.

For example:

set.seed(0)
df <- data.frame( y = rnorm(120), x = rep(c("a","b"), c(4,116)))
b <- bootstrap(df$y, mean(data[df$x == "a"]) - mean(data[df$x == "b"]),
              sampler = samp.permute, seed=1)
(1 + sum(b$replicates <= b$observed))/(b$B+1) # to calculate p-value

That syntax is ugly, in part because you need to permute only one
of the variables.  This is simplified with the new S+resample
library:

permutationTestMeans(df, treatment = x, alternative="less", seed=1)

See www.insightful.com/Hesterberg/bootstrap for info about the new library.
It includes a permutationTest() function for permutation tests other
than comparing two means.

Tim Hesterberg

P.S.  permutationTestMeans is fast, does 500K replications in 2.9 seconds
on a 650MHz Pentium.

