> 999; if not, you should be able to adjust my suggestion (which also assumes 
> that the data frame is called df and the variable in question temperature):
> 
>          df <- df[!(is.na(df$temperature) | df$temperature==999),]

Ummm ... not quite.  This will work fine for the rows with 999 values,
but for an NA value, the first term of the "OR" is T, the second is NA,
so the value is NA.  Subscripting with NA can cause some wierd results,
like getting rows of NA in your result.

You could do something like 

        df <- df[ifelse(is.na(df$tem),F,df$tem!=999),]

This is an easy mistake to make; NA doesn't act like a number in
comparisons so you have to think through their effects anywhere they might
appear.

