From stefano.sofia at usa.net  Tue May  6 15:38:49 2003
From: stefano.sofia at usa.net (Stefano Sofia)
Date: Tue, 06 May 2003 16:38:49 +0100
Subject: Summary about how deleting some rows from a dataframe
Message-ID: <243HeFPMx4400S06.1052235529@cmsweb06.cms.usa.net>

Thanks to Lambert Winnie, Robert Dodier, John Fox, Renaud Lancelot, Chuck
Cleland, Brian Ripley, Patrick Burns and Alan Zaslavsky.

I solved my (trivial) problem and I got some hints for other things also.
Here is the summary of the replies I received.
Thank you
Stefano

The command “is.na” is the core of each solution (apart from Patrick
Burns’s suggestion).

Lambert Winnie and similarly Robert Dodier suggested to create a new dataframe
like
!is.na(dataFrame$Temp)& dataFrame$Temp > -999;

John Fox suggested to create a new dataframe like
df[!(is.na(df$temperature) | df$temperature==999),]

Renaud Lancelot suggested
Idx <- apply(mat, 1, function(x) any(is.na(x) | x == 999)) 
and then
mat[!idx, , drop = FALSE]

Chuck Cleland suggested to create a new dataframe like
newdata <- mydata[is.na(mydata$temp)==FALSE & mydata$temp != -999,]

Brian Ripley suggested the example given in MASS4:
to use %in% rather than ==, 
as that does do what people expect. (%in% is only in S-PLUS 6.1 and R and
equivalent with is.element in S-PLUS >= 4. 
The example is

painters[is.element(School, c("A", "B", "D")), ]
painters[School %in% c("A", "B", "D"), ] ## R and S+6.1 

Only followed by a warning to consider the effect of NAs.)

Patrick Burns’s suggestion makes on use of “is.na”:
df[ df == -999 ] <- NA
where the data frame is called "df"; then just
df.nomiss <- na.omit(df)

Thanks also to Alan Zaslavsky and the discussion with John Fox and Brian
Ripley.

My Original Question was:
Dear Splus users,
I have a dataframe of more than 80.000 rows and 4 columns.
One of these columns stores temperature values (°C); sometimes there is NA
or
–999, 
values that I cannot axcept for my analysis. 
Is there an efficient way to recreate the original dataframe discarding all
