From marcv at water.ca.gov  Wed May  7 17:06:23 2003
From: marcv at water.ca.gov (=?utf-8?q?Vayssi=C3=A8res=2C_Marc?=)
Date: Wed, 07 May 2003 10:06:23 -0700
Subject: SUM: Seasonal decomposition of a time series with a few missing values
Message-ID: <30CA44F956F0E545825A119B6FE2DD0C085E30@SACEX3.ad.water.ca.gov>

SUMMARY:

 

I received the 
following answers to the question I posted to this list a 
little while ago. I have not had time to thoroughly test these ideas yet, but 
thanks to all.

-------------------------

>From Sean 
Keenan:

 

I would suggest fitting the series with a 
regression based on a sum of three or four cosine functions, where the 
parameters to be estimated are the amplitudes and phase coefficients of each cos 
function. You can use nls(). In fact, you can figure out what the phase 
coefficients should be in advance by inspecting the spectrum of the series. The 
beauty of this approach is that the independent variable is just 't', the time 
index for the months, so missing values won't affect the regression at all. 
Probably 60 months will be sufficient to obtain a good local fit. In any case, 
armed with this fit, you could replace missing values before filtering with 
stl.

 

-------------------------

>From Rick 
Ditmars:

 

I would think an 
interpolation of missing values would be better than arbitrary substitutions. 
The interpolation could be based on monthly or seasonal variations you 
see in the data. For an example see;

 

Lettenmaier, Dennis 
P. 1976. Detection of trends in water quality data from records with dependent 
observations. Water Resources Research. v12 n5, October, 
1976.

 

------------------------ 

 

From other discussions with 
colleagues:

 

If just a few of the monthly values are missing, 
replace them with the median value for that particular 
month.

 

------------------------

 

My question was:

 

 I would like to use the stl() function 
(Seasonal-Trend Decomposition using Loess) on long term environmental data. I 
have monthly time series over 25-30 years, with a few values missing because of 
equipment failure or accidental loss of the data.  

 

The 
S-Plus 6.1 implementation of the stl() function will not accept a time 
series with NAs. I was told by Insightful tech support that currently there 
is no way to set up stl() so that it will "smooth" over the missing 
values. I thought this would not be unreasonable since stl is using a 
sequence of loess smoothings. 

 

Using 
the na.omit function to remove 
the NA values from the time series is not 
a valid solution because stl() 
does a seasonal decomposition of the time series and periodic position in the 
time series is very important. For instance 
if the value for the month of 
March 1983 is missing, na.omit removes it and then shortens 
the time series so the value for April 1983 becomes that of March 1983, and so 
forth. 

 

I was 
told that a suitable work around 
(if there is not too many 
missing values) would be to just 
replace the NA's with a large number. Since stl() is robust, it will ignore these values in 
the algorithm, and essentially treat them as missing. What do you think of this solution? Do any of you have 
a better idea? 

 

 Marc 
Vayssières

