Sincere thanks to Tony Plate, Patrick Burns, Nick Ellis, and Bert Gunter for
helping with my question.  I learned how to do the intended task properly
and why my earlier method gave inconsistent results.

Since a dataframe is a list, I was assigning a single element rather than
the same value multiple times.  To do the task properly I must explicitly
assign the value over the full length of the dataframe component using:

flowfile <- cbind(flowfile, min.flow=.022)   or
flowfile[,"min.flow"] <- 0.022   or
flowfile$min.flow <- rep(0.022, numRows(flowfile)

Things got interesting when I tried to view my original results due to
different print methods.  Bert Gunter and Nick Ellis explained what was
happening "behind the scenes."  Here are the details from Bert:

I think it is the following: As any data.frame is a list, so is flowfile. If
you print it as a list via as.list(flowfile) or merely print the minflow
component via flowfile$minflow, you will find that the minflow component
contains one element. However, a data.frame must have columns = components
of the same length, so when you invoke the print.data.frame method, it must
fill out the column somehow. However, because of your ambiguous
specification of the minflow component, there is ambiguity in how this is
done. print.matrix() copies the existing value into all the rows for
printing. The data.frame subscripting function, "[.data.frame" treats(and
fills) the empty rows as NA's (missings). You can check that this is the
case by:

flowfile<-flowfile[1:nrow(flowfile),]
flowfile

You now will have NA's in the empty positions that will print as such in the
call to print.data.frame. SO the problem is inconsistent treatment of the
empty rows in the two functions. Interestingly, R does the same thing.

