In-Reply-To: <20030514120734.824A14021AE@smtp.biostat.wustl.edu>
References: <20030514120734.824A14021AE@smtp.biostat.wustl.edu>
Message-ID: <5.0.0.25.2.20030514092957.018ec878@pilot.msu.edu>

Herb,

The basic approach taken by a GIS to dissolve vectors between contiguous 
polygons is to identify a polygon attribute that is the same between the 
polygons.  I do not have an S-Plus to do this however the GIS process 
involves several of the following steps.

1. Assigning each vector a unique value and linkage to both contiguous 
polygons.

2. Subsetting and selecting all polygons from the main file with an equal 
attribute value.

3. Determining which vectors divide those polygons.

4. Subsetting the vectors that have to be eliminated based step number 3.

5. Finding the beginning x,y coordinate and the ending x,y coordinate for 
each subset vector.

6. Deleting the vector.

In S-Plus my hunch is that you will need at least 2 tables, one each for 
the polygon attributes, and one for the vector attributes.  I'm basically 
defining how ARC/INFO the leading GIS does this process.  The polygon 
attribute table should include a column(s) for the attribute(s) and the 
polygon id value.  The vector table then needs to have a linking attribute 
to the polygon data table so unique polygons are identifiable and 
selectable.  Additionally, the vector file needs information that defines 
the beginning and ending coordinates for the bisecting vector.

The reason you need two tables instead of one is that polygon tables must 
store information about the defined area.  Since you working with a school 
district these might include, number of pupils, single parent homes, and 
others (my imagination is not very good today).  These attributes allow you 
to find all polygons in the entire map that are identical.  The vector data 
table only includes information that can link the vectors back to specific 
polygons. In GIS systems this includes left polygon, right polygon, from 
node (xy), to node (xy) and several others.

If you can design that in S-Plus my complements. If anyone has implemented 
this in S-Plus I would also like to see the script.

Steve

