From Bill.Venables at csiro.au  Sat May 17 01:56:56 2003
From: Bill.Venables at csiro.au
Date: Sat, 17 May 2003 11:56:56 +1000
Subject: Re: matrix average by element
Message-ID: <E09E527B56BE2D438A3D6A246DDD27A9165B65@roper-cv.qld.cmis.CSIRO.AU>

If you can afford to copy the matrices a fairly conventional solution would
be

Mmean <- function(...) {
        mats <- list(...)
        A <- array(unlist(mats), dim = c(dim(mats[[1]]), length(mats)))
        apply(A, c(1,2), mean, na.rm = T)
}

> a <- matrix(1:4, 2, 2,)
> b <- matrix(5:8, 2, 2)
> d <- matrix(9:12, 2, 2)
> Mmean(a,b,d)
     [,1] [,2] 
[1,]    5    7
[2,]    6    8
> d[1,1] <- NA
> Mmean(a,b,d)
     [,1] [,2] 
[1,]    3    7
[2,]    6    8

A single-loop solution with the same interface as the above, using John
Fox's suggestion would be (untested code):

Mmean <- function(...) {
    mats <- list(...)
    tot <- n <- array(0, dim = dim(mats[[1]]))
    for(j in seq(along = mats)) {
                wh <- is.na(mats[[j]])
                n <- n + wh
                tot <- tot + ifelse(wh, 0, mats[[j]])
        }
        tot/n
}

I would expect this to be competitive with the first solution, particularly
in the case where the matrices were large compared to the number of them.

Bill Venables.

