From lambert.winnie at ensco.com  Fri May 23 21:25:28 2003
From: lambert.winnie at ensco.com (Lambert.Winnie)
Date: Fri, 23 May 2003 17:25:28 -0400
Subject: Summary: Gumbel Distribution
Message-ID: <8986151694190742869D08450EE4DCDE2B9CE8@amu-exch.ensco.win>

Thanks much to Sam 
Buttrey, Ravi Varadhan, Rolf Turner, and Simon Rosenfeld.  All inputs were 
helpful, but the suggestion that held the clue needed by my co-worker was from 
Rolf:

 

Your co-worker may have omitted the normalizing constants. The relevant fact 
is that if

X_(n) = max{X_1, ..., X_n}

where the X_i are iid random variables from a distribution

``in the domain of attraction'' of the Gumbel distribution then

P((X_(n) - a_n)/b_n <= x) ---> G(x) = exp(-exp(-x))

as n ---> infinity, where the a_n and b_n are ``normalizing constants'' 
which depend on the particular distribution that the X_i come from.

The normalizing constants for the standard normal distribution are:

ln(4 pi) + ln ln n

a_n = sqrt(2 ln n) - -------------------

2(sqrt(2 ln n))

1

b_n = ------------------

sqrt(2 ln n)

That is: If you repeatedly generate samples of size n (where n is

``large'') from a N(mu,sigma^2) distribution, standardize these samples (form 
Z_i = (X_i - mu)/sigma), take the max of the Z_i, and then form G = (Z_max - 
a_n)/b_n and thereby get a whole bunch of G's, then the histogram of the G's 
will look very much like the graph of G(x) = 
exp(-exp(-x)).

 

